## Provenance — what produced this, and what breaks if I change it

`tools/provenance.py` reads the dependency graph out of the code: which script
wrote each file, what it read, back to the raw inputs. Nothing is declared; it
works because every path goes through the roots above.

```bash
python3 -m tools.provenance --file <artifact>     # where did this come from
python3 -m tools.provenance --script <script>     # what must be re-run
python3 -m tools.provenance --mermaid <sub>       # draw it
python3 -m tools.provenance --orphans             # inputs nothing produces
```

**Run `--script` before changing anything upstream** and report the blast radius
with the change. Working that set out from memory is how a stale number survives
into a draft.

**Record runs that produce a reportable result:**

```bash
python3 tools/runlog.py <script> [args...]
```

It runs the script normally and records what it actually opened — an audit hook
below pandas and pyarrow, so paths built at runtime are caught. One line per run
in `project/<author>/logs/runs.jsonl`, committed: script, arguments, commit,
duration, status, inputs, outputs. Read them back with `--runs`.

The static graph is a strong first draft of provenance; the run records are what
actually happened.

---

## The event log — exhaustive, and not for you to read forward

Every prompt you are given and every tool call you make, yours and your
subagents', is appended to `project/<author>/logs/events-<run>.jsonl` by the hook
in `tools/log_event.py`. Debate calls land in the same file. Secrets are redacted
on the way in.

It writes itself. Three rules govern how you treat it:

1. **Never curate it and never read it forward as context.** It is not memory —
   `state.md` is the truth and `session.md` is the handoff. A log read back into
   a context window is the fastest way to fill one with things that have already
   been superseded.
2. **When a question genuinely needs it, read a slice, never the file.** It is
   line-delimited JSON; filter to what was asked for.

   ```bash
   python3 -m tools.logging_ --tool Bash --limit 20     # the last 20 shell calls
   python3 -m tools.logging_ --kind UserPromptSubmit    # what was actually asked
   python3 -m tools.logging_ --run <author>-<date>-<time>
   ```
3. **It is gitignored, so it is yours alone.** Another coauthor's log is not on
   your machine and never will be. Anything that must survive for everyone
   belongs in `state.md`, in the run records, or in the draft.

The questions it answers are the ones nothing else can: what exactly was run on
the afternoon a number changed, what the debate was actually asked, which file a
script opened when it produced a figure nobody can now reproduce. Those come up
rarely, and when they do nothing else will answer them.

A run is stamped with `python3 -m tools.runid --project . --new` at the top of a
round. Logs are named by run, never by round: a round counter is per-machine, so
in a shared repo two people each have a round 3, and `<author>-<date>-<time>`
cannot collide.

---

## One round at a time

`state.md` is long prose rewritten at every gate, and git cannot merge that. So
the round is claimed, and git enforces it — a push is atomic, so exactly one
claim wins.

```bash
python3 -m tools.lock claim "what this round is"
python3 -m tools.lock status
python3 -m tools.lock release
```

Claim before reading state; release after writing it, even if the round was
killed — an unreleased lock blocks everyone.
